<h3 id=3D"bpe-chooses">BPE chooses</h3>
<p><code>One + Plus</code></p>
<p>because 100 is greater than 40.</p>
<h3 id=3D"wordpiece-chooses">WordPiece chooses</h3>
<p><code>i + Pod</code></p>
<p>because <code>i</code> and <code>Pod</code> occur almost exclusively tog=
ether. Meanwhile, <code>One</code> and <code>Plus</code> are common tokens =
that frequently occur in many other contexts.</p>
<p>Therefore:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>BPE:</strong> =E2=80=9CDoes this pair occur often?=E2=80=9D</li=
>
<li><strong>WordPiece score:</strong> =E2=80=9CDoes this pair occur togethe=
r unusually often relative to its parts?=E2=80=9D</li>
</ul>
<p>It is still frequency-based, but it measures <strong>association</strong=
>, rather than raw frequency.</p>
<div style=3D"height:100px"></div>
<h2 id=3D"sentencepiece">SentencePiece</h2>
<p><strong>SentencePiece is not a third merge rule like BPE or WordPiece.</=
strong> Its major distinction is <strong>how it treats the input text</stro=
ng>.</p>
<h3 id=3D"the-key-idea">The key idea</h3>
<p>Traditional tokenizers often do this first:</p>
<pre style=3D"background-color:#22272e;color:#adbac7"><code><span><span>"th=
e cat"</span></span>
<span><span>=E2=86=92 ["the", "cat"]</span></span></code></pre>
<p>They split on spaces, then learn subwords inside each word.</p>
<p>SentencePiece instead treats the sentence as one raw character stream an=
d converts spaces into a normal visible symbol, usually <code>=E2=96=81</co=
de>:</p>
<pre style=3D"background-color:#22272e;color:#adbac7"><code><span><span>"th=
e cat"</span></span>
<span><span>=E2=86=92 "=E2=96=81the=E2=96=81cat"</span></span></code></pre>
<p>Possible tokens might be:</p>
<pre style=3D"background-color:#22272e;color:#adbac7"><code><span><span>["=
=E2=96=81the", "=E2=96=81cat"]</span></span></code></pre>
<p>or:</p>
<pre style=3D"background-color:#22272e;color:#adbac7"><code><span><span>["=
=E2=96=81", "the", "=E2=96=81ca", "t"]</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The <code>=E2=96=81</code> means:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A space occurred before this token.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Because whitespace is encoded explicitly, detokenization is straightforw=
ard:</p>
<pre style=3D"background-color:#22272e;color:#adbac7"><code><span><span>["=
=E2=96=81the", "=E2=96=81cat"]</span></span>
<span><span>=E2=86=92 "=E2=96=81the=E2=96=81cat"</span></span>
<span><span>=E2=86=92 " the cat"</span></span></code></pre>
<p>The initial space is typically removed.</p>
